/*! \page developer_backend_device_page Device Arrays

\ref developer_backend_build_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_math_page   (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

In code that uses a GPU, arrays may be allocated either in the main
memory of the CPU host or in the global memory of the GPU device. 
Arrays that are allocated in GPU memory when using a CUDA backend are 
declared in PSCF as instances of specializations of a class template 
named Pscf::DeviceArray. The class template Pscf::DeviceArray takes 
two template paramters, and is declared as:
\code
   template <typename Data, class T> class DeviceArray
\endcode
The argument of template parameter Data is the type of each element 
in the array. The argument of parameter T must be a backend identifer 
class. Partial specializations of the DeviceArray template with 
T=Pscf::CUT and T=Pscf::CPT are defined to be used with a CUDA GPU 
backend or a C++ CPU backend, respectively.

The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CUT is a wrapper
for an underlying array that is allocated in global GPU device memory,
which is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cuda/DeviceArray.h.
An instance of the class Pscf::DeviceArray<double,Pscf::CUT> is thus 
an array of double precision numbers that is allocated in GPU memory. 
Within code that is defined in the Pscf namespace, such an array would 
normally be defined as an instance of class DeviceArray<double,CUT>. 

The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CUT is instead a
wrapper for a conventional array that is allocated in CPU host memory. 
This is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cpp/DeviceArray.h. Within 
the Pscf namespace, an instance of class DeviceArray<double,CPT> is thus 
an array of double precision numbers that is allocated in CPU memory, 
similar in mamy respects to a Util::DArray<double>. One technical 
difference between a DeviceArray<Data,CPT> and a DArray<Data> is that 
DeviceArray<Data,CPT> is a subclass of a class Pscf::FftwArray that 
allocates memory aligned so as to guarantee efficent use by the FFTW 
fast Fourier transform library. 

Each specialization of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=CPT is indirectly 
derived from a specialization of Util::Array<Data> with the same 
typename Data. The Util::Array template provides an interface for access 
to individual elements on the CPU host via a subscript operator.  
Specializations of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=CUT do not allow access 
to individual elements from the CPU host, however, and instead only 
allow array elements to be accessed within CUDA kernels.

The definition of DeviceArray as a class template that takes a backend
identifier class as a template parameter T allows DeviceArray to be used 
in backend-independent template code within the definitions of other 
class templates that also take T as a template parameter.  In such 
templates, an instance of DeviceArray<Data,T> can be used to represent 
an array with elements of type Data that should be allocated on the GPU 
when using a CUDA backend (T=CUT) but that should allocated on the CPU 
when using a C++ backend (T=CPT). 

<BR>
\ref developer_backend_build_page   (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
